Relevant tables / columns and why they matter  

US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.US_ADDRESSES  
• "STATE": restrict rows to Florida (‘FL’)  
• "ZIP": link every address to a 5‑digit ZIP Code  
• "LATITUDE": rank addresses from north→south (higher latitude = farther north)  
• "NUMBER": first part of the street address we must return  
• "STREET": street name we must return  
• "STREET_TYPE": street type we must return  

US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_INDEX  
• "GEO_ID", "GEO_NAME", "LEVEL": identify Florida’s state‑level GEO_ID (needed for joins)  

US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_RELATIONSHIPS  
• "GEO_ID": ZIP‑code (e.g., zip/32615) we want to analyse  
• "RELATED_GEO_ID": links that ZIP to the Florida state GEO_ID (geoId/12)  
• "LEVEL": makes sure the record we pull is a ZIP (usually “CensusZipCodeTabulationArea”)  

US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_CHARACTERISTICS  
• "GEO_ID": same ZIP‑code string as above  
• "RELATIONSHIP_TYPE": want ‘coordinates_wkt’ so we can turn each ZIP polygon into a GEOGRAPHY object  
• "VALUE": polygon WKT used to calculate surface area → find “largest ZIP area”  

Typical conditions we will apply  
• "STATE" = 'FL'                                  -- for Florida addresses  
• "RELATIONSHIP_TYPE" = 'coordinates_wkt'         -- to obtain polygon WKT  
• "RELATED_GEO_ID" = 'geoId/12'                   -- Florida’s state GEO_ID (verified in query #1)  
• "GEO_ID" LIKE 'zip/%'                           -- keep only ZIP‑level polygons  
• ORDER BY ST_AREA(...) DESC                      -- rank ZIPs by area  
• ORDER BY "LATITUDE" DESC                        -- rank addresses north→south  



10 exploratory → final Snowflake SQL queries  
(Each returns ≤ 20 rows; all column names in double quotes.)

```sql
-- 1. Description: Confirm Florida’s state-level GEO_ID in the index table.
SELECT 
    "GEO_ID", 
    "GEO_NAME", 
    "LEVEL"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_INDEX
WHERE "GEO_NAME" ILIKE '%Florida%' 
  AND "LEVEL" = 'State'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Show a few ZIP GEO_IDs that are explicitly related to Florida.
SELECT DISTINCT 
    "GEO_ID",
    "LEVEL",
    "RELATED_GEO_ID",
    "RELATED_GEO_NAME"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_RELATIONSHIPS
WHERE "RELATED_GEO_ID" = 'geoId/12'        -- Florida
  AND "GEO_ID" LIKE 'zip/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Peek at ZIP polygons (WKT) that belong to Florida to ensure they exist.
SELECT 
    c."GEO_ID",
    LEFT(c."VALUE", 120) AS "WKT_SAMPLE"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_CHARACTERISTICS c
JOIN US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_RELATIONSHIPS r
  ON c."GEO_ID" = r."GEO_ID"
WHERE r."RELATED_GEO_ID" = 'geoId/12'
  AND c."RELATIONSHIP_TYPE" = 'coordinates_wkt'
  AND c."GEO_ID" LIKE 'zip/%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Compute area (m²) of every Florida ZIP polygon and show 20 largest.
SELECT 
    c."GEO_ID",
    ST_AREA( ST_GEOGFROMTEXT(c."VALUE") ) AS "ZIP_AREA_M2"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_CHARACTERISTICS c
JOIN US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_RELATIONSHIPS r
  ON c."GEO_ID" = r."GEO_ID"
WHERE r."RELATED_GEO_ID" = 'geoId/12'
  AND c."RELATIONSHIP_TYPE" = 'coordinates_wkt'
  AND c."GEO_ID" LIKE 'zip/%'
ORDER BY "ZIP_AREA_M2" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Isolate the single largest‑area Florida ZIP (top 1) for later use.
SELECT 
    c."GEO_ID",
    ST_AREA( ST_GEOGFROMTEXT(c."VALUE") ) AS "ZIP_AREA_M2"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_CHARACTERISTICS c
JOIN US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_RELATIONSHIPS r
  ON c."GEO_ID" = r."GEO_ID"
WHERE r."RELATED_GEO_ID" = 'geoId/12'
  AND c."RELATIONSHIP_TYPE" = 'coordinates_wkt'
  AND c."GEO_ID" LIKE 'zip/%'
ORDER BY "ZIP_AREA_M2" DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Count how many Florida addresses fall inside each of the 20 largest ZIP areas.
WITH big_zips AS (
    SELECT 
        c."GEO_ID"
    FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_CHARACTERISTICS c
    JOIN US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_RELATIONSHIPS r
      ON c."GEO_ID" = r."GEO_ID"
    WHERE r."RELATED_GEO_ID" = 'geoId/12'
      AND c."RELATIONSHIP_TYPE" = 'coordinates_wkt'
      AND c."GEO_ID" LIKE 'zip/%'
    ORDER BY ST_AREA( ST_GEOGFROMTEXT(c."VALUE") ) DESC
    LIMIT 20
)
SELECT 
    SUBSTR(b."GEO_ID", 5) AS "ZIP",
    COUNT(*)               AS "ADDRESS_CNT"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.US_ADDRESSES a
JOIN big_zips b
  ON a."ZIP" = SUBSTR(b."GEO_ID", 5)
WHERE a."STATE" = 'FL'
GROUP BY SUBSTR(b."GEO_ID", 5)
ORDER BY "ADDRESS_CNT" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Display a sample of addresses inside the single largest‑area ZIP.
--    Replace '32615' with the ZIP code found in query #5 if different.
SELECT 
    "ADDRESS_ID",
    "NUMBER",
    "STREET",
    "STREET_TYPE",
    "CITY",
    "LATITUDE",
    "LONGITUDE"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.US_ADDRESSES
WHERE "STATE" = 'FL'
  AND "ZIP" = '32615'          -- placeholder: use actual ZIP from query #5
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Rank Florida addresses in that ZIP north → south (highest latitude first).
--    Again, substitute correct ZIP code obtained earlier.
SELECT 
    "NUMBER",
    "STREET",
    "STREET_TYPE",
    "LATITUDE"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.US_ADDRESSES
WHERE "STATE" = 'FL'
  AND "ZIP" = '32615'
ORDER BY "LATITUDE" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Retrieve the 10 northernmost addresses (final answer set).
--    Ensure ordering strictly by latitude, returning only requested columns.
SELECT 
    "NUMBER"       AS "ADDRESS_NUMBER",
    "STREET"       AS "STREET_NAME",
    "STREET_TYPE"  AS "STREET_TYPE"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.US_ADDRESSES
WHERE "STATE" = 'FL'
  AND "ZIP" = '32615'           -- use largest-area ZIP found in query #5
ORDER BY "LATITUDE" DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Quick sanity‑check — confirm all 10 rows really lie inside the
--     geometry polygon for that ZIP (geospatial containment test).
WITH zip_poly AS (
    SELECT 
        ST_GEOGFROMTEXT(c."VALUE") AS "GEOG"
    FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.GEOGRAPHY_CHARACTERISTICS c
    WHERE c."GEO_ID" = 'zip/32615'               -- adjust to real GEO_ID
      AND c."RELATIONSHIP_TYPE" = 'coordinates_wkt'
)
SELECT 
    a."NUMBER",
    a."STREET",
    a."STREET_TYPE",
    a."LATITUDE"
FROM US_ADDRESSES__POI.CYBERSYN.US_ADDRESSES a,
     zip_poly z
WHERE a."STATE" = 'FL'
  AND a."ZIP" = '32615'
  AND ST_CONTAINS(z."GEOG", ST_POINT(a."LONGITUDE", a."LATITUDE"))
ORDER BY a."LATITUDE" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

Use the ZIP code surfaced in query #5 in place of the placeholder ‘32615’.  
Query #9 returns the final list of the ten northernmost addresses (number, street name, type) inside Florida’s largest ZIP‑code area.
